# Capital — Budget App

## Project Overview

Capital is an offline-first personal finance Android app. Monorepo layout:

```
android/    — Kotlin + Jetpack Compose (Phase 1, active)
backend/    — Python + FastAPI (Phase 2, not started)
web/        — React + TypeScript (Phase 3, not started)
specs/      — All feature and technical specs (source of truth)
```

Current phase: **Phase 1 — Android offline only.**

## Spec-Driven Development

All behavior is defined in specs before implementation. Before writing any code:

1. Read the relevant feature spec: `specs/features/[feature]/requirements.md`
2. Read the solution design: `specs/features/[feature]/design.md`
3. Follow the task list exactly: `specs/features/[feature]/tasks.md`

## Required Reading

Before starting any task, read these files:
- `ARCHITECTURE.md` — package structure, layering rules, dependency directions
- `specs/features/[feature]/requirements.md` — feature behavior
- `specs/features/[feature]/design.md` — UI and implementation design
- `specs/features/[feature]/tasks.md` — task list (follow exactly)

**Do not invent behavior, add fields, or deviate from specs.** If a spec is ambiguous, stop and ask — do not assume.

Business rule IDs (BR-XX-NN) are canonical. Never rename or renumber them.

## Package Name

`com.dmariani.capital` — use this in all generated Kotlin files.

## Global Data Rules (non-negotiable)

- All primary keys: UUID v4 (TEXT), generated client-side
- All amounts: INTEGER in cents — never REAL, never float
- All dates: INTEGER Unix timestamp in UTC
- Soft delete only — `deleted_at` timestamp; never hard delete (BR-DI-01)
- `created_at` and `updated_at` on every entity (BR-DI-02)
- `workspace_id` FK on every financial entity
- `sync_status` (TEXT, nullable) and `last_synced_at` (INTEGER, nullable) on every entity — null in Phase 1, active in Phase 2
- **Derived values are never persisted**: account balance, goal progress, budget spent, net worth — always computed at query time

## Naming Conventions

- Kotlin files: PascalCase (e.g. `TransactionViewModel.kt`)
- Feature folders: lowercase hyphenated (e.g. `recurring-transactions/`)
- Entities: singular PascalCase (`Transaction`, not `Transactions`)
- IDs: never renumbered once assigned

## Strings and SharedPreferences

- All UX copy lives in `strings.xml` — no hardcoded strings in Kotlin
- All SharedPreferences keys defined in `PreferenceKeys` object (`core/data/`)
- All SharedPreferences access goes through `PreferencesDataSource` (`core/data/`)

## Git

- Conventional Commits format
- Feature branches: `feature/xxx`
- Spec changes and code changes in separate commits

### Git Commit Standards
- Follow Conventional Commits: feat:, fix:, chore:, docs:, refactor:
- Scope commits to the affected module: feat(android):, feat(ios):, feat(web):, feat(backend):, docs:
- The title must be under 50 characters and in the present tense
- Separate title from body with a blank line
- Use a bulleted list in the body for multiple changes
- Each commit must represent a single logical change
- Avoid mixing refactor + feature in the same commit
- Ensure commits are meaningful and reviewable

## What NOT to do

- Do not add fields not in the spec
- Do not persist derived values
- Do not write SharedPreferences directly — always use `PreferencesDataSource`
- Do not hardcode strings in Kotlin files
- Do not perform hard deletes
- Do not generate Phase 2 code (networking, sync, auth) during Phase 1 tasks

## Task Execution Protocol

When implementing any task:
- Follow the task spec exactly — do not add anything not specified
- Ask before making any architectural decisions not covered by the spec
- If anything is ambiguous, stop and ask — do not assume
- Once a task is complete, mark it as Done in the relevant `tasks.md` file